{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is indefensible on Popper's own terms and smuggles induction back in by implicitly asserting that past performance is a reliable guide to future reliability, reducing the methodology to psychological habit rather than logical rationality.","counterconditions":["refuted if a coherent account can be provided where preferring a better-corroborated theory is justified solely as a method to maximize the severity of future tests without any assumption that the theory will actually succeed or remain unfalsified in the future","refuted if Popper explicitly distinguishes 'corroboration' as a report of past performance versus 'probability' as a prediction of future frequency in a way that validates action without inductive inference","refuted if the claim that Popper's framework reduces to mere psychological habit fails to demonstrate that no non-inductive logical rule can govern the preference"],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"16c4445643674d71"},{"block":"3a5ebf3b2c2c80a0"},{"block":"1b948fe0dcf78cd9"}],"mechanism":"By advising scientists to prefer the better-corroborated theory for action, Popper implicitly relies on the inductive premise that survival of severe tests indicates future stability, creating a performative contradiction where the system collapses into crypto-induction whenever it leaves abstract methodology.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible because corroboration functions as a measure of informational content rather than likelihood; we choose the better-corroborated theory not because we think it will hold true, but because it offers the most detailed map currently available for further criticism and testing.","counterconditions":["refuted if it can be demonstrated that the 'informational content' or 'boldness' of a theory cannot be assessed independently of its probability of being true","refuted if it can be demonstrated that preferring a theory for its 'boldness' implicitly relies on the inductive assumption that a theory which has withstood attacks in the past is a better instrument for navigating the future","refuted if evidence shows researchers consistently choosing less established, riskier hypotheses over well-corroborated ones solely to accelerate falsification"],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"182a58bdd39650d7"},{"block":"0db48ad23b546413"},{"block":"19d7f4e301abfb17"}],"mechanism":"Preferring the bold, well-tested theory is a preference for the most efficient path to potential falsification. If the theory fails, we learn more than if a weaker theory fails. This makes the preference a methodological rule for maximizing learning potential, not a probabilistic claim about the world's stability.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference is defensible on Popperian terms through the asymmetry of risk: rejecting a well-corroborated theory in favor of a poorly tested one increases the likelihood of immediate error without offering compensatory increase in testability.","counterconditions":["refuted if it could be demonstrated that the concept of 'minimizing known unknowns' logically entails a probabilistic assessment of future performance","refuted if it can be shown that choosing a poorly tested theory does not carry a higher 'likelihood of error' within a strict Popperian framework where all unfalsified theories stand on equal footing","refuted if the 'defensive posture' is shown to be illusory because action inherently aims at future results, entailing an inductive commitment"],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"193e9093bea201f6"},{"block":"07785266ae5808f5"}],"mechanism":"Choosing the better-corroborated theory minimizes 'known unknowns' relative to alternatives. This is a defensive posture requiring no belief in the future validity of the theory, only a recognition of its current robustness having survived greater exposure to falsification.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5}],"simulation_proposals":[]}